WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- A real backup script: create, VERIFY, log, clean up old backups
- tar -tzf proves an archive is readable without extracting it
- tee -a prints to screen AND appends to a log file
- find -mtime +N -exec rm removes old backups automatically
- A backup is only proven good after a successful restore test
- Backups on the same disk don't protect against disk failure — copy off-machine too (rsync/scp)
- In crontab, % must be written as \%

COMMANDS I PRACTICED:
[ ] ran backup.sh successfully
[ ] inspected the archive with tar -tzf
[ ] restored into /tmp/restore_test
[ ] added the cron entry

fill this in after running the script yourself :
$ ./backup.sh
> 

$ ls -lh ~/backups
> 

$ tar -tzf ~/backups/backup_<date>.tar.gz
> 


